    "answer": {
      "short": "The coefficient of $x^2$ is zero, so the equation is linear. It has one distinct real root, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$.",
      "worked": "Here $a=0$, so the equation is linear. The quadratic discriminant root-count rule requires $a\\ne0$; Bob's calculation of $\\Delta=4$ is correct, but his conclusion does not apply.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2x-3&=0\\\\2x&=3\\\\x&=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{align*}$$\n\nThere is one distinct real root.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
